[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m Design, section 1 of 4: [1marchitecture and the module boundary.[0m

  New file [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m at the repo root, alongside [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (matching where the browser half already lives — [38;5;153msrc/[39m is the
  unrelated Node half, and mixing them would imply a shared codebase that doesn't exist).

  The dual-load problem: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m uses a plain [38;5;153m<script src>[39m tag, and the tests run under Node. Rather than introduce a
  bundler or convert the page to ESM, [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m ends with a conditional CommonJS export:

  [34mif[39m ([34mtypeof[39m module !== [31m"undefined"[39m && module.exports) {
    module.exports = { createPrefs, DEFAULTS };
  }

  In the browser that branch is skipped and [38;5;153mcreatePrefs[39m is a global, exactly like [38;5;153mlogin[39m and [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m are today.
  Under [38;5;153mrequire()[39m it exports normally. No build step, no dependency, and the existing loading pattern in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is
  untouched — I just add one [38;5;153m<script src="prefs.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  The alternative was switching the page to [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m. I'm avoiding it because ESM over [38;5;153mfile://[39m is blocked by CORS,
  so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly in a browser — which is how this fixture is usable at all right now — would break.

  [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m knows nothing about the DOM, the login form, or theming. It's a storage module with a [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m API. All the
  wiring to inputs and the [38;5;153m<body>[39m class lives in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. That keeps the piece with logic worth testing free of anything
  that needs a DOM to run.

  Does the boundary look right?

